[{"id":96596,"sender_id":25,"sender_full_name":"David_Corfield","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/7d52fbe20c8ac05886a296e9ee2159b1?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/Third.20generation.20HOTT.20talks/near/96596\">#1</a> Somehow I missed that Mike’s 3 talks were going ahead: Yesterday (28 April), 5 May and 12 May, details <a href=\"https://www.cmu.edu/dietrich/philosophy/hott/seminars/index.html\">here</a>.</p>","timestamp":1651227074,"subject":"Third generation HOTT talks","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":96597,"sender_id":25,"sender_full_name":"David_Corfield","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/7d52fbe20c8ac05886a296e9ee2159b1?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/Third.20generation.20HOTT.20talks/near/96597\">#2</a> Is there any ‘Trinitarian’ story to tell about the advantages of HOTT over the other generations of HoTT? Do the differences correspond to anything in category theory?</p>","timestamp":1651231489,"subject":"Third generation HOTT talks","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":96598,"sender_id":25,"sender_full_name":"David_Corfield","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/7d52fbe20c8ac05886a296e9ee2159b1?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/Third.20generation.20HOTT.20talks/near/96598\">#3</a> […] Question answered in the talk.</p>","timestamp":1651241944,"subject":"Third generation HOTT talks","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":96599,"sender_id":1008,"sender_full_name":"Madeleine Birchfield","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/f759fe50e1d01fe5f7869048a1e486cc?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/Third.20generation.20HOTT.20talks/near/96599\">#4</a> Is there a way to define dependent/homogeneous identity types without using universes?</p>","timestamp":1651271841,"subject":"Third generation HOTT talks","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":96600,"sender_id":1008,"sender_full_name":"Madeleine Birchfield","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/f759fe50e1d01fe5f7869048a1e486cc?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/Third.20generation.20HOTT.20talks/near/96600\">#5</a> I meant heterogoeneous identity types, rather than “homogeneous” identity types.</p>","timestamp":1651282282,"subject":"Third generation HOTT talks","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":96601,"sender_id":51,"sender_full_name":"Ulrik","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/e499ead5270df65e0b599d630ce0f40b?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/Third.20generation.20HOTT.20talks/near/96601\">#6</a> @4,5: There are many equivalent ways of defining dependent identity types, several of which don’t need universes. The simplest is perhaps as an identity type in the right (or left) fiber, using transport. (I.e., <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><mi>B</mi></msubsup><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>:</mo><mo>≡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></mrow><mi>P</mi></msup><mtext> </mtext><mi>p</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>a</mi><msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(a =^B_p b) :\\equiv (\\mathrm{trp}^P\\,p\\,a =_{B(b)} b</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.2244em;vertical-align:-0.3831em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\"><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8413em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.453em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">p</span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0502em;\">B</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3831em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:≡</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.2015em;vertical-align:-0.3552em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">trp</span></span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8463em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.068em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">P</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">p</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\"><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3448em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.5198em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0502em;\">B</span><span class=\"mopen mtight\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">b</span><span class=\"mclose mtight\">)</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3552em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span></span></span></span>, for <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a,b:A</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span></span></span></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>a</mi><msub><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi></msub><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p:a=_A b</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">p</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.5806em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\"><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3283em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">A</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span></span></span></span>, and <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0502em;\">B</span></span></span></span> a type family over <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span></span></span></span>.) But you can also express them as an indexed inductive type.</p>","timestamp":1651302426,"subject":"Third generation HOTT talks","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":96603,"sender_id":10,"sender_full_name":"Mike Shulman","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/ede34282f405f902e12fa7e6cf336572?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/Third.20generation.20HOTT.20talks/near/96603\">#7</a> I assume Madeleine’s question was about the specific heterogeneous identity types in H.O.T.T., since the latter was the subject of this thread.  One can simply postulate all the n-ary heterogeneous identity types, along with all their structure, rather than deriving them from n-ary ap and the universe.  Semantically, one may want to do that anyway, and then separately assert equalities saying that they happen to coincide with the underlying correspondences of ap into the universe.  But for a syntactic introduction, I find it clean and nicely motivating to derive them from the latter.</p>","timestamp":1651365611,"subject":"Third generation HOTT talks","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":96604,"sender_id":10,"sender_full_name":"Mike Shulman","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/ede34282f405f902e12fa7e6cf336572?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/Third.20generation.20HOTT.20talks/near/96604\">#8</a></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Is there any ’Trinitarian’ story to tell about the advantages of HOTT over the other generations of HoTT? Do the differences correspond to anything in category theory?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Roughly I would say that:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Book HoTT corresponds to Quillen model categories.</li>\n<li>Cubical type theories correspond to Quillen model categories containing an interval object.</li>\n<li>H.O.T.T. corresponds to Quillen model categories enriched over semicartesian cubical sets.  (This will become clearer after the second and third talks.)</li>\n</ol>\n<p>In all cases one needs extra niceness properties, but this is the basic distinguishing structure.  Does that go any way towards answering the question?</p>","timestamp":1651365749,"subject":"Third generation HOTT talks","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":96605,"sender_id":1008,"sender_full_name":"Madeleine Birchfield","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/f759fe50e1d01fe5f7869048a1e486cc?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/Third.20generation.20HOTT.20talks/near/96605\">#9</a> My question was about the heterogeneous identity types in Mike’s presentation of higher observational type theory. Mike’s presentation implicitly uses universes a la Russell, where every type is a term of a universe. But Mike’s definition of heterogeneous identity types through universes presents some problems in a type theory which uses universes a la Tarski: since it is not true that every type is a term of a universe a la Tarski, the given definition is only valid (after translating appropriately) for those types that are some coercion <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{El}(A)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">El</span></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span> of a term <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A:U</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.109em;\">U</span></span></span></span> of a universe <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.109em;\">U</span></span></span></span>.</p>","timestamp":1651368113,"subject":"Third generation HOTT talks","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":96609,"sender_id":10,"sender_full_name":"Mike Shulman","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/ede34282f405f902e12fa7e6cf336572?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/Third.20generation.20HOTT.20talks/near/96609\">#10</a> It works fine with universes a la Tarski as long as every type belongs to some universe.</p>","timestamp":1651378323,"subject":"Third generation HOTT talks","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":96610,"sender_id":10,"sender_full_name":"Mike Shulman","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/ede34282f405f902e12fa7e6cf336572?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/Third.20generation.20HOTT.20talks/near/96610\">#11</a> If you have types that don’t belong to any universe, then you’d have to give primitive rules for the heterogeneous equality types, as I suggested in <a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/Third.20generation.20HOTT.20talks/near/96603\">#7</a>.</p>","timestamp":1651378367,"subject":"Third generation HOTT talks","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":96663,"sender_id":25,"sender_full_name":"David_Corfield","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/7d52fbe20c8ac05886a296e9ee2159b1?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/Third.20generation.20HOTT.20talks/near/96663\">#12</a> Re <a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/Third.20generation.20HOTT.20talks/near/96604\">#8</a>, thanks Mike! Since you seem to be convinced that H.O.T.T. represents a philosophical improvement, perhaps I expected this to be clear in the direction of change of categorical semantics, say to something simpler. Maybe I shouldn’t expect this though.</p>","timestamp":1651607392,"subject":"Third generation HOTT talks","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":96805,"sender_id":1008,"sender_full_name":"Madeleine Birchfield","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/f759fe50e1d01fe5f7869048a1e486cc?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/Third.20generation.20HOTT.20talks/near/96805\">#13</a> Looks like Mike Shulman gave primitive rules for dependent identity types in his second talk yesterday.</p>","timestamp":1651820067,"subject":"Third generation HOTT talks","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":96818,"sender_id":43,"sender_full_name":"DavidRoberts","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/fbc2dbfb28f9e9275a4fd4cd109b5a21?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/Third.20generation.20HOTT.20talks/near/96818\">#14</a> Is there any reason the video is not yet available on YouTube? I think the first one was up quite promptly.</p>\n<p><strong>Edit</strong> Oh, I see Jonas Frey <a href=\"https://hott.zulipchat.com/#narrow/stream/236329-talk-announcements/topic/CMU.20HoTT.20Seminar.20Online.3A.20Shulman/near/281360297\">gave</a> a temporary “link for the impatient” here: <a href=\"https://u.pcloud.link/publink/show?code=XZXBjpVZ8cOAFR7oWoRbobS6NWsNE82pz3Ny\">https://u.pcloud.link/publink/show?code=XZXBjpVZ8cOAFR7oWoRbobS6NWsNE82pz3Ny</a>, the official upload will happen presumably before the day is out.</p>","timestamp":1651828983,"subject":"Third generation HOTT talks","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":96882,"sender_id":1008,"sender_full_name":"Madeleine Birchfield","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/f759fe50e1d01fe5f7869048a1e486cc?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/Third.20generation.20HOTT.20talks/near/96882\">#15</a> The video of the 2nd lecture has been posted to Youtube <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5ciDNfmvMdU\">here</a>.</p>","timestamp":1651889324,"subject":"Third generation HOTT talks","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":96883,"sender_id":43,"sender_full_name":"DavidRoberts","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/fbc2dbfb28f9e9275a4fd4cd109b5a21?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/Third.20generation.20HOTT.20talks/near/96883\">#16</a> Thanks, Madeleine!</p>","timestamp":1651889975,"subject":"Third generation HOTT talks","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":97141,"sender_id":1008,"sender_full_name":"Madeleine Birchfield","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/f759fe50e1d01fe5f7869048a1e486cc?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/Third.20generation.20HOTT.20talks/near/97141\">#17</a> For anybody interested, the third lecture has been posted to Youtube <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9pDddxB7LbE\">here</a>.</p>","timestamp":1652406041,"subject":"Third generation HOTT talks","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]}]